  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\nI'll develop a multi-stage optimization approach using coordinate descent with probabilistic diversification as the core. This uses methods from the previous program but adds three key innovations:\n- Structured initialization with domain-specific wavelet patterns instead of uniform sampling\n- Soft Nelder-Mead integration for structured local optimization\n- Anti-pattern detection to reject unstable approximations\nThe method leverages FFT-based convolution evaluation and combines coordination descent sweeps with dynamic wavelet synthesis. The rationale is that by starting with targeted patterns rather than random initialization, we can accelerate convergence toward minimal J values while the anti-pattern detection prevents noisy local minima from dominating the search space.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\nLet a = (a_0, a_1, ..., a_{n-1}) be a probability simplex vector (\u22650 sum=1). The objective is J(a) = 2*n*max(convolve(a,a)[k] for k) where convolve() is full convolution. The constraints are:\n- \u2211a_i \u2265 1 (after simplex projection)\n- a_i \u2264 1000\n- a_i must be real and finite\n\nWe use a mathematical duality approach:\nJ(a) = min(\u2225a\u2225\u221e, 2n/\u2211a_i\u00b2)\nThis allows us to separate the optimization into two parts:\n\u2022 Phase 1: Simplex projection to achieve sum=1\n\u2022 Phase 2: Golden-ratio optimization of patterns\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\nThe algorithm proceeds through four distinct phases:\n\na) Initialization:\n  1. Start with base_sequence_512 if available, else uniform sequence\n  2. Generate wavelet-informed candidates via:\n     - Gaussian bumps with controlled bandwidth\n     - Sinusoidal patterns with harmonic frequencies\n     - Wavelet pattern approximations (Morlet wavelets)\n  3. All sequences are projected to simplex (sum=1)\n\nb) Coordinate Descent phase:\n  1. Run CD with increasing sweep counts (up to 1000)\n  2. Monitor improvement plateau using geometric decay\n  3. Maintain two distinct CD implementations:\n     - One with random order perturbations (for global search)\n     - One with harmonic-centric sweeps (for iterative refinement)\n\nc) Wavelet pattern synthesis:\n  1. Generate patterns based on current solution\n  2. Use Morlet wavelet amplitudes to create candidate structures\n  3. Scale to simplex automatically via normalizing filter\n\nd) Anti-pattern safeguarding:\n  1. Monitor for excessive oscillation in CD\n  2. Record penalty scores for unstable patterns\n  3. Implement short-term termination for high-penalty candidates\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations:\nI'll implement a variation schedule with:\n- 20% initial candidates via wavelet synthesis\n- 80% optimization per target length\n- Dynamic hyperparameter adjustment for CD:\n  \u2022 lr = 0.03 + 0.02 * (current iteration % 10) \n  \u2022 simplex_size = 10 * (0.85^(iteration/100)))\n- Initial length distributions: \n  \u2022 Base: 512 points\n  \u2022 Shrink to: 256, 384, 640, 768, 1024 points via linear interpolation\n- The algorithm will use a golden section search for optimizing amplitude/width parameters\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\nTime allocation:\n- 75% for CD optimization (up to 1125 seconds)\n- 20% for wavelet pattern exploration\n- 5% for management/anti-pattern checks\n\nHypervolume usage:\n- Working list size \u2264 15 candidates (5 from training, 10 from exploration)\n- Candidate construction time: each sequence is 512 points unless specified otherwise\n\nFallback procedure:\nIf time runs out, return best-of-last-iteration, but force a final evaluation:\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\nAll candidates must be validated through:\na) Pre-evaluation check (1 second timeout)\nb) Final statistical set reduction (J improvements)\nc) Convergence report if plateau reached\nThe returned sequence is guaranteed:\n- Non-negative (a_i \u22650)\n- Floating point numbers\n- Sum \u2265 1 (after simplex projection)\nLet's code accordingly",
